## ⚠ Structural gap — decide separately from the content above

`verify_m*.js` harnesses exist for **M0–M6** and **M15–M20**. They do **not** exist for **M7–M14**:

```
present : m0 m1 m2 m3 m4 m5 m6 · m15 m16 m17 m18 m19 m20
missing : m7 m8 m9 m10 m11 m12 m13 m14
```

Eight middle modules with no verification harness — **including M13, the testing module itself.** So
the module that will teach *"prove your assertion can fail"* is one of the eight that has no
mechanism proving its own content holds.

**This is flagged, not planned.** Backfilling eight harnesses is a different-sized job than the
content additions above, and whether it is worth doing is a separate call. It is recorded here
because the irony makes it easy to notice now and easy to forget later.

---

## Cross-references — what goes WHERE

The same lesson was deliberately split across four destinations. Keeping the boundary is the point;
collapsing it is how project trivia ends up in general teaching material.

| destination | carries | constraint |
|---|---|---|
| Global `/dev-flow` command | the portable principle: assert against what the producer emits; every acceptance names and **executes** the mutation that reddens it | stack-free |
| Project `docs/engineering-rules.md` (s19_app) | markdown-it token types, entity spoofing, escaped-form ≠ character, code-span headings, AST-over-regex | project-bound; stays there |
| Skill `tui-design` → `VERIFY.md` | extends the existing `## Pin the truth, not a string` section | **general only** — must hold for ANY Textual/terminal project; examples de-identified |
| **This course (M13/M14/M17)** | the teaching version of both lessons, with worked examples | **may use s19_app examples in M13, which already declares it as anchor** |

**Note on the skill leg, worth knowing while writing M13.** `VERIFY.md` already says *"assert on a
runtime value, never on the presence of a label."* That rule is **necessary but not sufficient**, and
this batch produced the counterexample: a substring search over SVG source **is** a runtime value, it
satisfies the existing rule completely, and it returned 0/19 against a correct implementation. If M13
teaches only "assert on runtime values", it inherits the same hole. The teachable step is one level
deeper: *pin the truth in the form the producer emits it.*
